<node id="1435646651" lat="51.7622249" lon="-1.2699357"><tag k="HE_ref" v="1047105"/><tag k="access" v="yes"/><tag k="check_date" v="2025-02-26"/><tag k="disused:amenity" v="drinking_water"/><tag k="drinking_water" v="no"/><tag k="inscription" v="Drink and think of him who is the fountain of life"/><tag k="inscription:2" v="With the consent of the Lords of the Manor, this drinking fountain is erected by Mr. William Ward to mark the site of a celebrated spring known as Walton Well adjacent to the ancient fordway into Port Meadow called Walton Ford"/><tag k="listed_status" v="Grade II"/><tag k="name" v="Walton Well Drinking Fountain"/><tag k="nickname" v="Belmont Drinking Fountain"/><tag k="note" v="The &quot;Brumman's Well&quot; name was also given to Aristotle's Well, nearby. The name refers to &quot;Brumman le Rich or de Walton&quot; (see source). I can't find anything backing up the &quot;Belmont Drinking Fountain&quot; name."/><tag k="old_name" v="Bruman's Well"/><tag k="source" v="local_knowledge; http://www.oxfordhistory.org.uk/streets/inscriptions/north/drinking_fountain.html"/><tag k="source:old_name" v="Robert Charles Hope, _The legendary lore of the holy wells of England: including rivers, lakes, fountains and springs_, 1893 London Elliot Stock, https://archive.org/details/legendaryloreofh00hope/mode/2up (as &quot;Brumman's Well&quot;)"/><tag k="start_date" v="1885"/><tag k="tourism" v="attraction"/><tag k="wikidata" v="Q26299218"/></node>
